f 17592186045417] [17592186551173 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551173 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551173 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551173 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b2cf6229-1b40-568c-b2ae-1e954c9aa404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551233 #edb/ref 1069 "q-331789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551233 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551233 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a function $r(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{r(t)\\}=R(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551233 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "002b6494-db6e-5c52-baa6-f8349498d2c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551233 #edb/ref 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{3t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-3)t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $R(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-3)t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t=R(s-3),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-3>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175